#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cafa24e-4543-5b34-90d7-30a196dea194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868} {:answer/id #uuid "e11913eb-e3e1-5f57-a56a-942b4c1c6e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372869} {:answer/id #uuid "677af0cd-8fcf-515a-9ae8-5589651ec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372870} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca9f35e-fe96-5630-bbab-c22309b7bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cafa24e-4543-5b34-90d7-30a196dea194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868} :answer-field/id #uuid "426794d2-6862-5438-ba72-af2a254821f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28c90549-a363-5df9-b9c5-c5f06fe88f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344281" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-1,1]$, where $f$ is $2$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $2$-periodic on $[-1,1]$, we have $L=1$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca1a5ef5-d795-51cc-943c-7a342a6b79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372886} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d3a205-6dc6-5c9e-bac4-a249e8ab4de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372887} {:answer/id #uuid "2853c5b9-37c6-5227-b945-7d1f193fd0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372888} {:answer/id #uuid "83ee3b8e-a567-507f-a915-c7357bb35a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ee3b8e-a567-507f-a915-c7357bb35a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372889} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b894e19-8672-5d3a-ad64-9899ae78c522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1435ee5-1c1b-550d-94b4-e30eac47db5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344322" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{8}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{8}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 3 & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"}]}
